strategy.sequencing:
0. Decisions first: fork or upstream, judge strictness, the embedded model, platform scope, OOM policy, data compatibility, islands, shutdown semantics.

1. prompts/00b-judge-setup.md on this Mac.
Exit: two identical runs on the pinned C++, N of N passing with retries off, at least 10 injected mutations each caught, all parity families built, coverage map and performance baseline recorded, flaky cases quarantined with a stated reason.

2. Pin a C++ base commit, and sync by tree diff, not by commit message (bulk deletions have ridden in unrelated commits such as 45cbbe9e1 and 706253423). Replay upstream in scheduled windows only, never in the middle of a piece. Pin the judge corpus to the same commit: mysql_test went from 600 to 283 cases in six months, and 24 commits re-recorded plan rows in a year.

3. Seam refactors in C++, verified by the unchanged judge, offered upstream where possible:
(a) replace the raw sql::ObExpr, ObPushdownOperator and ObPushdownFilterExecutor pointers in ObVTableScanParam (src/data_plane/api/data_plane/access/ob_tablet_scan.h) with a filter interface that storage owns. This removes storage calling ob_pushdown_filter.cpp:1451, the inlined ObExpr::eval, and the sql to storage link cycle (271 and 100 symbols);
(b) remove the 4 include-cycle lines from d51422b54;
(c) introduce an explicit context object for the server_service<T> slots the first piece uses.

4. Steps 1-2 over the whole tree: map, core design document, periphery rulebook, inventory, stress test.

5. Pilot piece through Steps 3-6. The candidate is logservice/palf (32,705 lines). It is link-clean. Its seam is ObILogHandler plus the AppendCb (8 subclasses), ObIReplaySubHandler (14) and ObICheckpointSubHandler (15) callbacks. Every DML exercises it, and the restart family tests replay. Measure tokens per unit, check rate, whether failures can be traced, and the developer's attention. Wait for codex/palf-log-buffer-redesign to settle first, or pick the FTS tokenizers instead.

6. Piece A, the storage side: storage, tx, MDS, data_plane, logservice and standby (if in scope), running under C++ SQL. Its churn is lower: 9 ordinary storage commits in 30 days. The storage-estimation part of EST.ROWS gets checked against an unchanged C++ optimizer.

7. Piece B, the SQL side: sql, pl, query, rootserver, observer, share/schema. It goes last because it holds most of the text the judge pins and the highest churn (sql/optimizer and sql/rewrite). Switching it removes the last C++ callers of the C++ foundation.

Before any translation (Step 3), all of these must exist:
- the validated judge;
- the pinned base and sync script;
- a written mask policy;
- the coverage map, with every unexecuted unit either given a differential harness or listed as accepted-unverified;
- an acyclic crate plan with a measured per-crate check time;
- templates/settings.json installed by the developer;
- a per-step token ceiling with a stop rule.

alternatives.why_not:
It verifies best, because the judge runs from day one, but the core has no narrow seams:
- sql and storage call each other at link level (271 and 100 symbols), and storage compiles ObExpr/ObEvalCtx code inline;
- header-only templates appear in 290 api signatures;
- base-layer classes are subclassed across modules (ObTimerTask 72, ObDLinkBase 72, ObFuncExprOperator 313).
Every small seam forces C-compatible layouts that block the ownership redesign until the last C++ caller is gone. Unsafe code grows at every seam (sql-nio already has 186 unsafe blocks in 8.4K lines). Only one Rust staticlib can be linked. Mixed releases need byte agreement for clog and slog, whose checksums hash raw struct memory (log_entry_header.cpp:83). Many small seams mean many bridges that get thrown away later. I keep only its good part: few, designed seams, landed in C++ first.

It is the lowest-risk option, but the payoff is in the core's arena and async handoffs, so porting only leaves buys little. I adopt it only for vsag, S2, ICU, share/geo with boost, and the bison parser, and those still bring costs:
- a try/catch at every extern C entry (ob_geo_dispatcher.h:1314-1363 maps 18 exception types);
- the malloc hook must keep charging island allocations;
- no Rust frame may sit between parse_sql and a longjmp;
- on wasm, the islands force Emscripten and the JavaScript exception model.

