strategy.recommended:
Build the judge first. Then redesign the core as new Rust crates by hand. Then translate the rest into a parallel Rust tree in crate order and switch over at the end. Keep five C++ islands behind C ABIs.

The old C++ binary stays the product and the spec until the Step 6 gate.

The core crates are designed and written by the developer with the largest model, not by the fan-out:
- error type carrying the exact OB code;
- allocator and arena handles;
- string and datum views;
- containers;
- explicit context in place of THIS_WORKER, CURRENT_CONTEXT and the server_service slots;
- ExprId/StmtId arenas for the shared IR;
- typed column batches;
- Arc-shared schema snapshots;
- the tx context ownership.
Where possible they are proven with parity tests against C++. The precedent is origin/feature/rust's compare_cpp.py, which diffs 4,000 seeded cases bit for bit.

The islands:
- vsag;
- S2;
- share/geo with boost.geometry, behind a kernel API of about 76 entry points;
- ICU regex;
- the bison parser with its ObParser/ObFastParser layer.

Only the islands and the existing sql-nio cross FFI. No FFI bridges are built for code that will later be deleted.

alternatives.why_not:
The kit's mechanical rulebook cannot express the ownership model:
- 2,741 placement-new sites and 5,938 raw-pointer fields;
- pointer-identity expressions (about 580 find_item/set-helper lines);
- tablets that hold pointers into their own pooled buffers.
Every area verifier says the result would be wall-to-wall unsafe Rust that keeps the same bugs. You would pay about 10-55B processed tokens for Step 3 and get little safety back. The parallel-tree mechanics are kept in the recommended strategy; only the one-to-one ownership is dropped.

The judge pins OB-specific behavior a new design would not have:
- 1,546 errno entries;
- __all_* catalogs polled by tests;
- set_tp tracepoint IDs;
- cost-model numbers;
- MySQL-exact cast and arithmetic output (770,711 expected lines).
A rewrite would fail most of the 153 internals-bound cases, and no judge would say when it is done.

verdict.conditions:
Move to "migrate now" only when all six hold. Each is cheap next to the port, and most pay off even if the port never happens.

1. The 00b judge is built and validated: the local runner, differential mode, restart, --ps-protocol, embedded and performance baselines, and a measured coverage map. The frozen C++ passes N/N, and every hand-injected mutation is caught.

2. The developer has decided:
   - the platform scope;
   - the data-compatibility policy (a version bump plus logical export);
   - the embedded model (seekdb-bindings' 24-function out-of-process driver or feat/embedded-mode's 93-function in-process ABI);
   - whether wasm is in scope;
   - the upstream-sync policy;
   - the OOM policy.

3. The tree is frozen at a named commit after the parallel lines the developer wants are merged or declared out: feature/plugin with its 34.6K Rust lines, feat/embedded-mode, feature/webassembly-shell, feature/rust. Syncing from upstream seekdb then happens per PR at batch gates, or not at all.

4. A cargo check-rate and memory measurement on this Mac pins the 500-5,000 lines/s range to within 2x.

5. The Phase A-B pilot (hand-built substrate crates plus a parity-tested value library) shows real per-unit token use and time within the bands.

6. At least about 150 GB of disk is free.

Until then, spend only on the judge and the pilot.

decisions_for_human.why:
The C++ already refuses any other data version: ob_data_version_mgr.cpp:61, plus strict equality checks in persisted objects added in July 2026. Byte compatibility would add one codec unit per format family:
- palf headers whose checksums cover raw struct memory;
- the slog;
- the tx log;
- the super block, which is copied with MEMCPY;
- sstable headers with bitfields and a pointer/int union.
Each would need golden data directories written by a named commit.
